// error
// SPEC 10.3.1 (#1659): `hostTarget()` is NOT admissible inside a `@nosplit`
// body, and this case pins that refusal rather than a fix.
//
// It is the one primitive on the §11 list that reaches the runtime by a real
// call: `compiler/lowerprim.bit`'s `primRtName` maps it to `RtFn.host_target`,
// and codegen emits it through `rtSymbol` as `call bit_rt_host_target`. Every form
// §10.3.1 already admits is admitted because it is NOT a call — `syscall` and
// the atomics and `fsqrt` lower to inline instructions, `ptrOf`/`entryOf`/
// `stackMapsBegin` to a link-time address materialization. The single
// assertion-based entry, `extern function` (§11.7), rests on that keyword being
// itself an unmanaged-subset marker; `hostTarget` is an ordinary predeclared
// builtin callable from any managed Bit code and carries no such marker.
//
// The runtime needs the ordinal inside poll-free code all the same
// (`runtime/root/linux/fs.bit`'s `isX64`, `io.bit`'s `nr`). It gets it WITHOUT
// widening this guard, by asking a `@nosplit` sibling whose body is one `asm`
// immediate — see run_nosplit_asm_arch.bit for that shape, and
// `runtime/root/linux/os.bit`'s `rtHostTarget` for the real one. The ordinal is
// a property of the emit, so it never needed a runtime call.
@nosplit fn isX64(): bool {
  return hostTarget() == 0
}

fn main() {
  let unused = isX64()
}
